WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.634 --> 00:00:04.337
-Our next guest is a talented
actor you know from his work on

00:00:04.337 --> 00:00:06.472
shows such as "Supernatural"
and "Smallville."

00:00:06.472 --> 00:00:08.608
He joins the Emmy-nominated
series "The Boys,"

00:00:08.608 --> 00:00:10.376
which is now streaming
exclusively on

00:00:10.376 --> 00:00:11.678
Amazon Prime Video.

00:00:11.678 --> 00:00:12.812
Let's take a look.

00:00:12.812 --> 00:00:14.881
-The most idiotic of them all.

00:00:14.881 --> 00:00:15.982
-What do you say, Gunpowder?

00:00:15.982 --> 00:00:17.483
Give them the Fourth of July.

00:00:17.483 --> 00:00:18.751
-Let's light 'em up,
Soldier Boy.

00:00:18.751 --> 00:00:19.919
-Please put that down.

00:00:21.588 --> 00:00:23.623
-Oh, well, I didn't realize

00:00:23.623 --> 00:00:25.325
they made case officers
as pretty as you.

00:00:26.426 --> 00:00:28.194
We're just having some fun.

00:00:28.194 --> 00:00:29.762
-Our munitions dump
is in that direction.

00:00:29.762 --> 00:00:32.031
You're about to blow up
half the camp.

00:00:32.031 --> 00:00:34.667
Put it down -- now.

00:00:37.036 --> 00:00:38.671
-I do like the assertive type.

00:00:38.671 --> 00:00:42.442
You know, with a figure like
yours, you are wasted down here.

00:00:42.442 --> 00:00:45.445
-Please welcome to the show
Jensen Ackles, everyone.

00:00:45.445 --> 00:00:47.480
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:00:59.258 --> 00:01:00.760
Such a pleasure to
have you here.

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:03.730
How you doing?
-Hey. Good. Great. Grand.

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:06.098
-I am a big fan of this show.
This show is gory.

00:01:06.098 --> 00:01:07.166
This show is graphic.

00:01:07.166 --> 00:01:09.402
This show is also
very funny at times.

00:01:09.402 --> 00:01:11.070
Were you familiar with it
before they reached out?

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:12.104
-I was.

00:01:12.104 --> 00:01:15.641
Eric Kripke,
who's the showrunner,

00:01:15.641 --> 00:01:18.711
created a little show a few
years ago called "Supernatural."

00:01:18.711 --> 00:01:19.945
-Yeah.

00:01:19.945 --> 00:01:25.384
-And so I was certainly
friends with the boss.

00:01:25.384 --> 00:01:28.721
And when he came out with
the show, I was like,

00:01:28.721 --> 00:01:30.422
"I'm gonna have to watch
another Kripke show."

00:01:30.422 --> 00:01:31.991
'Cause he's done -- You know,
he went off and did "Timeless."

00:01:31.991 --> 00:01:33.059
He went off
and did "Revolution."

00:01:33.059 --> 00:01:35.227
The guy --
He can't stop working.

00:01:35.227 --> 00:01:39.131
But this one really is him
with the gloves off.

00:01:39.131 --> 00:01:40.633
And it shows.

00:01:40.633 --> 00:01:42.168
Because it is everything

00:01:42.168 --> 00:01:44.303
that he couldn't do
on network television.

00:01:44.303 --> 00:01:46.339
-It is -- You have
a million-dollar voice

00:01:46.339 --> 00:01:47.807
for superheroes,

00:01:47.807 --> 00:01:51.277
and I feel like the clip was
a perfect illustration of that.

00:01:51.277 --> 00:01:54.347
But you have done some
superhero voice work before.

00:01:54.347 --> 00:01:57.016
Not acting, but you did some
DC Comic stuff.

00:01:57.016 --> 00:01:59.485
You did Red Hood.
-Yep.

00:01:59.485 --> 00:02:02.021
In fact, I just spent about
3 1/2 hours in the studio

00:02:02.021 --> 00:02:04.557
yesterday doing Batman.

00:02:04.557 --> 00:02:06.092
-Oh, wow.
You do it for Batman, too?

00:02:06.092 --> 00:02:08.060
Look at that.
That's a big deal.

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:10.330
-That is a big deal.
I don't want to drop that ball.

00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:12.131
-Yeah. Now, that, of course,

00:02:12.131 --> 00:02:14.467
is nice because
you just get to do the voice.

00:02:14.467 --> 00:02:17.370
Here, you have to get
the gear on.

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:21.307
It seems like as far as
the costumes on this show go,

00:02:21.307 --> 00:02:23.409
you did okay,
'cause some of them are

00:02:23.409 --> 00:02:24.877
maybe a little bit less fun
to put on.

00:02:24.877 --> 00:02:30.015
-Yeah, it was -- I got the
nice one, I would --

00:02:30.015 --> 00:02:31.417
I can still move around.

00:02:31.417 --> 00:02:33.786
There was no leotard, no rubber,

00:02:33.786 --> 00:02:35.821
no suit that you had to peel off
at the end of the day.

00:02:35.821 --> 00:02:36.990
-Right.

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:39.058
-That being said,
while we were fitting it --

00:02:39.058 --> 00:02:40.459
'Cause these things
are works of art

00:02:40.459 --> 00:02:43.763
and they take quite a bit of
time to really put together.

00:02:43.763 --> 00:02:46.765
The super-suit
costume designer, LJ --

00:02:46.765 --> 00:02:49.135
she was starting to put
the stuff together and was like,

00:02:49.135 --> 00:02:51.837
"Are you gonna give me some --
you know, give me some muscles?"

00:02:51.837 --> 00:02:54.306
Like, I see these suits,

00:02:54.306 --> 00:02:57.476
and they are clearly outlined
with, you know,

00:02:57.476 --> 00:02:58.577
added muscles.

00:02:58.577 --> 00:02:59.646
-No, I would definitely show up

00:02:59.646 --> 00:03:01.346
assuming muscles
would be there already.

00:03:01.346 --> 00:03:04.383
-Yeah. And then -- But instead,
she just lightly taps me

00:03:04.383 --> 00:03:05.617
on the shoulder and she goes,

00:03:05.617 --> 00:03:08.721
"You're going to bring me
some muscles in April."

00:03:08.721 --> 00:03:11.290
-Wait. How much time --
When was that?

00:03:11.290 --> 00:03:12.992
When was that conversation?
-This was like October.

00:03:12.992 --> 00:03:14.860
-Oh, my God.
That's not enough time.

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:16.328
-Well, it was.
-Yeah. Oh, wow.

00:03:16.328 --> 00:03:18.498
-I was like,
"Challenge accepted."

00:03:18.498 --> 00:03:20.599
-Yeah. I'm sorry that I saw
the clip and I was like,

00:03:20.599 --> 00:03:23.269
"That wasn't enough time."

00:03:23.269 --> 00:03:24.970
-Yeah, thanks, man.

00:03:24.970 --> 00:03:28.041
So, apparently, it wasn't.

00:03:30.276 --> 00:03:31.311
-You mentioned that
this is a show --

00:03:31.311 --> 00:03:33.779
-Noddle-armed Soldier Boy there.

00:03:33.779 --> 00:03:36.181
-This is --
Obviously, I would say

00:03:36.181 --> 00:03:38.084
there's not an episode
in the show that you don't see

00:03:38.084 --> 00:03:39.518
five or 10 examples of something

00:03:39.518 --> 00:03:40.987
that couldn't be on
network television.

00:03:40.987 --> 00:03:43.689
Obviously, you mentioned you're
friends with the showrunner.

00:03:43.689 --> 00:03:45.458
Did he -- Was there anything
he asked you to do

00:03:45.458 --> 00:03:48.227
that you thought, "Oh, man,
this is not friendship"?

00:03:48.227 --> 00:03:50.863
-So, yeah, if anybody's ever
thinking of coming on to

00:03:50.863 --> 00:03:55.902
a Kripke show and portraying
one of his characters,

00:03:55.902 --> 00:03:58.037
never tell him
that something bothers you.

00:03:58.037 --> 00:04:01.440
-Oh, interesting.
-He will write to it full force.

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:03.108
-Oh, like, he'll -- If you have,
like, one fear thing --

00:04:03.108 --> 00:04:04.143
-Oh, yeah, if you're like,

00:04:04.143 --> 00:04:07.546
"I don't like fluid
all over me,"

00:04:07.546 --> 00:04:10.316
he's like, "Oh, okay!

00:04:10.316 --> 00:04:12.418
Okay. Got it."

00:04:12.418 --> 00:04:14.887
-Yeah. So if you do it,
be like, "I hate cake."

00:04:14.887 --> 00:04:16.356
-Right, right, right, right.

00:04:16.356 --> 00:04:19.391
Needless to say,
he found my line very quickly

00:04:19.391 --> 00:04:20.693
and then continued to
write to it.

00:04:20.693 --> 00:04:23.496
-So, this is
a no-spoiler situation,

00:04:23.496 --> 00:04:24.931
but for anybody
who finishes the season,

00:04:24.931 --> 00:04:26.966
we should be able to pinpoint
the moment where you --

00:04:26.966 --> 00:04:29.668
he has found the weakness?

00:04:29.668 --> 00:04:33.740
-Yeah, I mean, let's --
Yeah, without any spoilers,

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:36.909
I mean, it's just
odd sexual positions.

00:04:36.909 --> 00:04:38.911
-Alright, great.

00:04:38.911 --> 00:04:40.612
Now, that is troubling,
'cause that means,

00:04:40.612 --> 00:04:41.781
based on everything
you've said so far,

00:04:41.781 --> 00:04:43.082
that you went to him and said,

00:04:43.082 --> 00:04:46.319
"There's just -- I got
two sexual positions I hate.

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:49.055
Whatever you do, Kripke,

00:04:49.055 --> 00:04:51.357
just know this one
and this one are off limits."

00:04:51.357 --> 00:04:53.326
-Well, they --

00:04:53.326 --> 00:04:57.596
And this is how really advanced
this whole process is,

00:04:57.596 --> 00:05:03.168
is I would get e-mails with
just stick figures in positions.

00:05:03.168 --> 00:05:05.204
I would be like,
"Oh, 'A' and 'B' are out.

00:05:05.204 --> 00:05:07.673
I'm sorry. I can't.
I have children."

00:05:07.673 --> 00:05:08.741
-That's right.

00:05:08.741 --> 00:05:10.342
You do --
And we were talking backstage.

00:05:10.342 --> 00:05:12.544
We each have three kids.
You've got two 5s and a 9.

00:05:12.544 --> 00:05:13.679
-I got two 5s and a 9.

00:05:13.679 --> 00:05:16.182
-So, this is obviously
just off limit.

00:05:16.182 --> 00:05:18.183
They cannot watch this show.
-Oh, yeah, no.

00:05:18.183 --> 00:05:20.085
-Is there anything --
I mean, they must be aware.

00:05:20.085 --> 00:05:22.188
-My parents don't watch
this show.

00:05:22.188 --> 00:05:25.057
My mom better not.

00:05:25.057 --> 00:05:26.826
-Is there anything
that you could show them

00:05:26.826 --> 00:05:29.328
where they think, "Oh, that's
cool that dad's doing that"?

00:05:29.328 --> 00:05:31.330
-So, a few years back,

00:05:31.330 --> 00:05:33.065
"Supernatural" did
a crossover with "Scooby-Doo."

00:05:33.065 --> 00:05:34.200
-Alright, perfect.

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:39.204
-And, I mean,
they were animating me

00:05:39.204 --> 00:05:40.573
as my character on
"Supernatural,"

00:05:40.573 --> 00:05:42.407
but at the end of the day,
they were just animating me.

00:05:42.407 --> 00:05:45.678
So dad is animated
with Scooby-Doo on a cartoon

00:05:45.678 --> 00:05:47.213
that my kids just adore.

00:05:47.213 --> 00:05:49.148
-And you got to voice it?
-Oh, yeah.

00:05:49.148 --> 00:05:50.182
-Oh, that's good.
-Yeah.

00:05:50.182 --> 00:05:51.884
-And they weren't
thrown off by that?

00:05:51.884 --> 00:05:54.020
Because I have some friends
who have done voice-over work,

00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:56.422
and they say their kids --
they don't like that moment.

00:05:56.422 --> 00:05:59.224
-Well, 'cause I have
actor friends

00:05:59.224 --> 00:06:04.130
that do voices of characters in
movies that they have watched

00:06:04.130 --> 00:06:05.331
that they're very familiar with.

00:06:05.331 --> 00:06:07.533
And I'll be like,
"Hey, hey, talk to my buddy."

00:06:07.533 --> 00:06:11.570
And then they're like, "Why is
that person talking to me?"

00:06:11.570 --> 00:06:13.372
So -- Or they'll come over to
the house and be like,

00:06:13.372 --> 00:06:15.507
"This is so-and-so,"
and they're like,

00:06:15.507 --> 00:06:20.980
"No, that doesn't look anything
like a little dog."

00:06:20.980 --> 00:06:22.648
-Yeah. Well, that's
the -- I've said the same thing,

00:06:22.648 --> 00:06:23.850
where I'm like,
"I've actually --

00:06:23.850 --> 00:06:25.050
Yeah, the guy
who does that bear voice --

00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:26.152
he's been on my show."

00:06:26.152 --> 00:06:27.219
And I show them a clip,
and they're like,

00:06:27.219 --> 00:06:28.353
"Oh, he's a human."

00:06:28.353 --> 00:06:30.856
-Unimpressed.
Unimpressed at this point.

00:06:30.856 --> 00:06:32.992
-I do want to mention --

00:06:32.992 --> 00:06:36.062
You know,
you alluded to "Supernatural."

00:06:36.062 --> 00:06:38.130
15 years, over 300 episodes.

00:06:38.130 --> 00:06:40.499
This is, like, an unheard of
accomplishment for

00:06:40.499 --> 00:06:43.001
you, yourself, and everybody
who did that show.

00:06:43.001 --> 00:06:45.404
And, obviously,
an incredible fan base.

00:06:45.404 --> 00:06:47.773
I don't think any show could be
on 15 years without one.

00:06:47.773 --> 00:06:49.708
That must have been
a tremendous ride.

00:06:49.708 --> 00:06:50.842
-It was.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:50.842 --> 00:06:52.979
Thank you. Yeah.
-Give it up.

00:06:55.147 --> 00:06:58.651
-Yeah, I think we topped out
at 327 episodes

00:06:58.651 --> 00:07:03.321
of prime-time television,
which was a long, long run.

00:07:03.321 --> 00:07:04.757
And it was fantastic.

00:07:04.757 --> 00:07:06.625
There was a reason why I think
we all stuck around, though,

00:07:06.625 --> 00:07:07.860
because we just
truly enjoyed it.

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:09.428
It was a family.

00:07:09.428 --> 00:07:11.597
You know, we --
I spent birthdays,

00:07:11.597 --> 00:07:14.533
and we spent holidays
and we -- There was just --

00:07:14.533 --> 00:07:17.403
We went through so much together
as a crew and a cast

00:07:17.403 --> 00:07:20.472
that by the end of it,
it was a really sad goodbye,

00:07:20.472 --> 00:07:21.874
but something that we all felt
like we really earned.

00:07:21.874 --> 00:07:23.042
-That's really cool.
-Yeah.

00:07:23.042 --> 00:07:26.145
-A shorter run,
but also a big fan base --

00:07:26.145 --> 00:07:28.280
you did three years on
"Days of Our Lives."

00:07:28.280 --> 00:07:31.483
-[ Laughs ]
Nobody remembers that.

00:07:31.483 --> 00:07:33.286
-Do you get recognized, though?

00:07:33.286 --> 00:07:36.689
-So, okay, funny story. No.

00:07:36.689 --> 00:07:38.690
-Okay.

00:07:38.690 --> 00:07:40.793
-Until about two weeks ago.
-Okay.

00:07:40.793 --> 00:07:42.360
-When I was on
a flight back from Europe --

00:07:42.360 --> 00:07:44.330
we were over there doing some
press for "The Boys,"

00:07:44.330 --> 00:07:49.335
and a lovely flight attendant
kind of, like --

00:07:49.335 --> 00:07:51.938
You know how they look at you
and they're like, "Ehhh..."

00:07:51.938 --> 00:07:54.807
-Yeah.
-Like, "Ehhhhhh."

00:07:54.807 --> 00:07:57.309
So I'm thinking
maybe "Supernatural,"

00:07:57.309 --> 00:07:58.443
maybe "The Boys," I don't know.

00:07:58.443 --> 00:08:00.913
And, so, I'm coming out of
the bathroom,

00:08:00.913 --> 00:08:02.481
and they're there in the galley.

00:08:02.481 --> 00:08:07.520
And he says,
"Days of Our Lives."

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:09.555
And I immediately was like,

00:08:09.555 --> 00:08:12.425
"You're not even old enough
to remember me."

00:08:12.425 --> 00:08:14.059
And, immediately,
he went right into it.

00:08:14.059 --> 00:08:16.562
He was like,
"What's Marlena like?"

00:08:17.730 --> 00:08:20.732
And, so, I sat there speaking,
you know, like,

00:08:20.732 --> 00:08:22.602
taking up space in the galley
there, sipping coffee.

00:08:22.602 --> 00:08:24.937
-Well, I mean,
I would imagine -- For 15 years,

00:08:24.937 --> 00:08:26.705
you've had to answer so many
questions about "Supernatural."

00:08:26.705 --> 00:08:29.375
It must have been a nice break
to go back to the "Days" days.

00:08:29.375 --> 00:08:30.943
-Yeah, I had to go through
the files in the brain there

00:08:30.943 --> 00:08:32.411
and come up with some stories.

00:08:32.411 --> 00:08:35.381
But, yeah, David, if you're out
there, that was a pleasure.

00:08:35.381 --> 00:08:36.515
It was enjoyable.

00:08:36.515 --> 00:08:38.083
-And I'm sure, your next flight,

00:08:38.083 --> 00:08:39.485
you'll be answering questions
about "The Boys."

00:08:39.485 --> 00:08:40.553
Hey, thanks so much
for being here.

00:08:40.553 --> 00:08:42.088
It's been a real pleasure
talking to you

00:08:42.088 --> 00:08:43.522
and having you on.

00:08:43.522 --> 00:08:44.957
Jensen Ackles.

00:08:44.957 --> 00:08:47.326
The first three episodes of
the third season of "The Boys"

00:08:47.326 --> 00:08:49.694
are streaming now, with
new episodes available Fridays

00:08:49.694 --> 00:08:50.829
on Amazon Prime Video.

00:08:50.829 --> 00:08:52.398
We'll be right back
with music from Tove Lo.

